<pretext>
    <article xml:id="worksheet">

    <!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
        <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="hw-hw01">
            <title>Chapter 1 Homework and Writing Problems.</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    These are the homework problems for Chapter 1. For due dates for different sections of the book, see Blackboard.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Section 1.1: 14 and 34
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Section 1.2: 4, 6, 8, 13
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The matrices for 6 and 8 are described on the previous page.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Section 1.3: 2, 11, 22, 24
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Section 1.4: 1, 3, 17
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Section 1.5 (Writing): 9, 11, 14;
                    </p>
                    <p><alert>Extra 1.</alert> 
                        The textbook proves the proposition, <q>For all <m>n \times n</m> matrices <m>A</m> and <m>m \times 1</m> matrices <m>\mathbf{b}</m>, if <m>A</m> is nonsingular, then <m>A \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}</m> has a unique solution.</q> However, it is not written to conform to the style expectations of your professor. Rewrite the proof, making sure to:
                        <ul>
                        <li><p>Make sure you define your notation (<q>Let ...</q>).</p>
                        </li>
                        <li><p>State your assumptions (<q>Suppose...</q>).</p>
                        </li>
                        <li><p>Use the definition of nonsingular to tell your reader that a specific matrix exists.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Starting with <m>A^{-1}\mathbf{b} = A^{-1}(A\mathbf{x})</m>, work your way to <m>\mathbf{x}</m> without using a <q>two-column</q> proof. That is, given a long enough line, you should be able to write one long sequence of equalities on one line.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p><alert>Extra 2.</alert> 
                        Write the logical inverse of <q>if <m>A</m> is nonsingular, then <m>A \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}</m> has a unique solution.</q>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
        </worksheet>
    </article>
</pretext>
